What is AOP?

AOP = Aspect-Oriented Programming

It is a way to add extra behavior (logging, security, timing, transactions)
to your methods WITHOUT putting that code inside every method.

Normal OOP → put logic inside the class/method
AOP       → put common logic outside, and Spring applies it automatically

Think of it like this:

Your business method = the main work (create user, place order)
AOP aspect           = extra work around it (log, check auth, measure time)

Without AOP (messy):

public String getUsers() {
    log.info("API called");          // extra
    // business logic
    log.info("API finished");        // extra
    return "Users list";
}

With AOP (clean):

public String getUsers() {
    return "Users list";             // only business logic
}
// logging happens automatically via Aspect


Key AOP Terms (simple)

Aspect     → the class that holds the extra logic (@Aspect)
Advice     → when to run that logic (@Before, @After, @Around, etc.)
Pointcut   → which methods to target (e.g. all controller methods)
JoinPoint  → the actual method being intercepted


Simple flow:

Client calls API
      ↓
Aspect runs (@Before / @Around...)
      ↓
Controller / Service method runs
      ↓
Aspect runs again (if @After / @Around)
      ↓
Response returned


Why AOP exists:

Some concerns are needed in many places:
- Logging
- Security
- Performance timing
- Transactions
- Auditing

These are called Cross-Cutting Concerns
(they cut across many classes).

AOP lets you write them once and apply them everywhere.


🔑 Interview One-Liner

AOP (Aspect-Oriented Programming) allows you to separate cross-cutting concerns
like logging and security from business logic, and apply them automatically
to selected methods using aspects and advice.


--------------------------------------------------


Here is a real production example used in microservices built with Spring Boot to log every HTTP request automatically using AOP.

This is very common in backend systems.

1️⃣ Problem

In microservices we have many controllers:

UserController
OrderController
PaymentController
ProductController

If we log manually:

log.info("Request received");

inside every API method, it becomes repetitive and messy.

So we use AOP to log all controller requests automatically.

2️⃣ Create Logging Aspect
@Aspect
@Component
public class ControllerLoggingAspect {

    @Before("execution(* com.example.controller..*(..))")
    public void logRequest(JoinPoint joinPoint) {

        String methodName = joinPoint.getSignature().getName();
        String className = joinPoint.getTarget().getClass().getSimpleName();

        System.out.println("API Called: " + className + "." + methodName);
    }
}
What this means
execution(* com.example.controller..*(..))

This means:

any method
inside controller package
in any class

3️⃣ Example Controller
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/users")
public class UserController {

    @GetMapping
    public String getUsers() {
        return "Users list";
    }
}
4️⃣ What Happens When API is Called

Client calls:

GET /users

Console output:>>>>>>>>>>>>>

API Called: UserController.getUsers

Your controller code remains clean.

5️⃣ Even Better Production Example

You can log:

URL

HTTP method

request parameters

execution time

Example:

@Around("execution(* com.example.controller..*(..))")
public Object logExecutionTime(ProceedingJoinPoint joinPoint) throws Throwable {

    long start = System.currentTimeMillis();

    Object result = joinPoint.proceed();

    long time = System.currentTimeMillis() - start;

    System.out.println(joinPoint.getSignature() + " executed in " + time + "ms");

    return result;
}

Example output:>>>>>>>>>>>>.

UserController.getUsers executed in 15ms


6️⃣ Why Companies Use This

In microservices there may be 50+ APIs.

AOP helps to:

log all requests

measure performance

add security checks

audit API calls

without modifying controller code.

🔑 Interview Answer (Very Important)

In Spring Boot, AOP can be used to log all HTTP requests by creating an aspect that intercepts controller methods using pointcut expressions
 like execution(* controller..*(..)), allowing centralized logging without modifying each API method.

 🔑🔑🔑🔑🔑🔑Difference between

 @Before
 @After
 @AfterReturning
 @AfterThrowing
 @Around

 In AOP of Spring Framework / Spring Boot, these annotations define when an aspect should run relative to the target method execution.

 They are called Advice types.

 1️⃣ @Before

 Runs before the method executes.

 Example
 @Before("execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))")
 public void logBefore() {
     System.out.println("Method is about to run");
 }
 Flow
 @Before
 ↓
 Actual Method

 Example Output

 Method is about to run
 Creating user
 Use Cases

 Logging

 Authentication

 Input validation

 2️⃣ @After

 Runs after the method finishes, whether it succeeds or throws an exception.

 Example
 @After("execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))")
 public void logAfter() {
     System.out.println("Method finished");
 }
 Flow
 Method
 ↓
 @After

 Even if exception happens, this still runs.

 3️⃣ @AfterReturning

 Runs only if the method executes successfully.

 Example
 @AfterReturning(pointcut="execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))", returning="result")
 public void logSuccess(Object result) {
     System.out.println("Method returned: " + result);
 }
 Flow
 Method
 ↓
 Return value
 ↓
 @AfterReturning
 Use Case

 Logging response

 Caching result

 4️⃣ @AfterThrowing

 Runs only when the method throws an exception.

 Example
 @AfterThrowing(pointcut="execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))", throwing="ex")
 public void logException(Exception ex) {
     System.out.println("Exception occurred: " + ex.getMessage());
 }
 Flow
 Method
 ↓
 Exception
 ↓
 @AfterThrowing
 Use Case

 Error logging

 Alerting system

 5️⃣ @Around (Most Powerful)

 Runs before AND after the method.

 You can even control whether the method runs or not.

 Example
 @Around("execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))")
 public Object logExecutionTime(ProceedingJoinPoint joinPoint) throws Throwable {

     long start = System.currentTimeMillis();

     Object result = joinPoint.proceed();

     long time = System.currentTimeMillis() - start;

     System.out.println("Execution time: " + time + " ms");

     return result;
 }
 Flow
 @Around (before)
 ↓
 Method
 ↓
 @Around (after)
 Use Cases

 Performance monitoring

 Transaction handling

 Security checks

 Retry logic

 🔑 Simple Comparison Table
 | Advice            | When It Runs                        |
 | ----------------- | ----------------------------------- |
 | `@Before`         | Before method execution             |
 | `@After`          | After method (success or exception) |
 | `@AfterReturning` | Only after successful return        |
 | `@AfterThrowing`  | Only when exception occurs          |
 | `@Around`         | Before and after method             |

 🔑 Interview One-Line Answer

 In Spring AOP, @Before, @After, @AfterReturning, @AfterThrowing, and @Around are advice types that define
 when an aspect executes relative to the target method lifecycle.

